1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,710
It's two in the morning, and your phone won't

2
00:00:02,730 --> 00:00:03,770
stop buzzing.

3
00:00:03,870 --> 00:00:04,580
Payment failed.

4
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:05,470
Payment failed.

5
00:00:05,490 --> 00:00:07,830
Late fee. Your bank account is in the negative.

6
00:00:07,910 --> 00:00:09,710
And somewhere in there is a reminder that

7
00:00:09,730 --> 00:00:10,900
your groceries…

8
00:00:10,919 --> 00:00:12,110
are past due.

9
00:00:12,310 --> 00:00:13,839
Yep. That's you.

10
00:00:13,870 --> 00:00:15,080
Twelve "pay in four"

11
00:00:15,099 --> 00:00:17,149
plans. Across three apps.

12
00:00:17,169 --> 00:00:19,640
Two thousand, three hundred and forty dollars

13
00:00:19,660 --> 00:00:22,809
you owe… on stuff you mostly don't even remember

14
00:00:22,829 --> 00:00:24,879
buying. And the crazy part?

15
00:00:24,899 --> 00:00:26,879
It all started with a sixty-dollar pair of

16
00:00:26,899 --> 00:00:28,589
sneakers. No interest.

17
00:00:28,609 --> 00:00:29,679
No credit check.

18
00:00:29,699 --> 00:00:31,079
Four easy payments.

19
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:33,199
So here's what we're figuring out today.

20
00:00:33,219 --> 00:00:35,039
Somewhere between that first sixty-dollar

21
00:00:35,059 --> 00:00:36,169
plan and this…

22
00:00:36,189 --> 00:00:38,159
there was one purchase where it stopped being

23
00:00:38,179 --> 00:00:40,199
a shortcut and became a trap.

24
00:00:40,320 --> 00:00:41,500
Which one was it?

25
00:00:41,899 --> 00:00:43,379
Let's go back to the beginning.

26
00:00:47,393 --> 00:00:49,524
Level one. One plan.

27
00:00:49,793 --> 00:00:51,314
You're scrolling late at night.

28
00:00:51,493 --> 00:00:53,704
Sneakers. Sixty dollars.

29
00:00:53,843 --> 00:00:56,033
And right under the price, there's a button:

30
00:00:56,274 --> 00:00:58,453
four payments of fifteen dollars.

31
00:00:58,503 --> 00:01:00,003
Zero percent interest.

32
00:01:00,243 --> 00:01:02,823
Your brain does something very human here.

33
00:01:02,914 --> 00:01:04,924
It stops seeing sixty dollars.

34
00:01:04,944 --> 00:01:06,634
It only sees fifteen.

35
00:01:06,823 --> 00:01:08,723
And fifteen dollars is nothing.

36
00:01:09,203 --> 00:01:11,134
Fifteen dollars is a sandwich.

37
00:01:11,293 --> 00:01:13,593
And signing up takes about thirty seconds.

38
00:01:13,674 --> 00:01:15,853
Name, phone number, debit card.

39
00:01:15,903 --> 00:01:17,623
Usually no hard credit check.

40
00:01:17,783 --> 00:01:20,853
Approved. The first fifteen comes out today.

41
00:01:20,943 --> 00:01:23,134
The other three come out automatically, every

42
00:01:23,153 --> 00:01:25,114
two weeks, straight from your card.

43
00:01:25,213 --> 00:01:26,993
You don't have to remember anything.

44
00:01:27,144 --> 00:01:29,223
That's the part that feels like magic.

45
00:01:29,443 --> 00:01:31,243
Tap. And honestly?

46
00:01:31,283 --> 00:01:33,914
At this level, buy now, pay later is kind

47
00:01:33,933 --> 00:01:36,993
of… fine. You pay it back on time, you pay

48
00:01:37,013 --> 00:01:38,184
zero interest.

49
00:01:38,204 --> 00:01:39,174
That's the deal.

50
00:01:39,233 --> 00:01:41,474
Plenty of people use it exactly like this,

51
00:01:41,493 --> 00:01:43,323
and nothing bad ever happens.

52
00:01:43,493 --> 00:01:45,823
So who's paying for your free money?

53
00:01:46,024 --> 00:01:47,183
Mostly the store.

54
00:01:47,304 --> 00:01:49,594
When you use pay in four, the store usually

55
00:01:49,614 --> 00:01:51,524
pays the app a fee on the sale.

56
00:01:51,603 --> 00:01:53,594
And stores are happy to pay it, because people

57
00:01:53,614 --> 00:01:55,823
who can split a price tend to buy more…

58
00:01:55,843 --> 00:01:57,114
and buy bigger.

59
00:01:57,343 --> 00:01:59,304
Your friend Jay likes the sneakers.

60
00:01:59,503 --> 00:02:01,433
You like how easy that was.

61
00:02:01,554 --> 00:02:02,783
That's level one.

62
00:02:02,853 --> 00:02:05,403
One plan. Totally under control.

63
00:02:06,414 --> 00:02:08,463
Level two. Two plans.

64
00:02:08,734 --> 00:02:10,794
Two weeks later, there's a jacket.

65
00:02:11,003 --> 00:02:12,723
A hundred and twenty dollars.

66
00:02:12,813 --> 00:02:14,174
Four payments of thirty.

67
00:02:14,473 --> 00:02:15,944
But here's the thing.

68
00:02:15,984 --> 00:02:17,734
The sneakers aren't paid off yet.

69
00:02:17,963 --> 00:02:20,463
This is the first quiet change, and it's

70
00:02:20,484 --> 00:02:22,104
the one nobody notices.

71
00:02:22,253 --> 00:02:24,183
You're not paying for A thing anymore.

72
00:02:24,243 --> 00:02:26,473
You're paying for a stack of things, on different

73
00:02:26,493 --> 00:02:28,984
schedules, from different weeks of your life.

74
00:02:29,213 --> 00:02:30,274
Fifteen here.

75
00:02:30,294 --> 00:02:31,294
Thirty there.

76
00:02:31,484 --> 00:02:33,514
Each one feels small on its own.

77
00:02:33,634 --> 00:02:35,384
That's how it's designed to feel.

78
00:02:35,533 --> 00:02:37,774
But on Friday, both come out of your account

79
00:02:37,794 --> 00:02:39,004
at the same time.

80
00:02:39,304 --> 00:02:41,033
Let's actually do the math here, because

81
00:02:41,054 --> 00:02:42,623
nobody ever does.

82
00:02:42,764 --> 00:02:45,193
Two plans means eight payments, spread over

83
00:02:45,213 --> 00:02:47,484
about two months, landing on four different

84
00:02:47,504 --> 00:02:50,323
days. Some weeks it's fifteen dollars.

85
00:02:50,343 --> 00:02:52,163
Some weeks it's forty-five.

86
00:02:52,334 --> 00:02:54,644
And you didn't choose any of those dates.

87
00:02:54,704 --> 00:02:56,593
They were picked for you, the second you

88
00:02:56,614 --> 00:02:57,623
tapped the button.

89
00:02:57,864 --> 00:03:00,993
That's the quiet cost of buy now, pay later.

90
00:03:01,114 --> 00:03:02,353
It's not the interest.

91
00:03:02,394 --> 00:03:03,984
It's that your future paychecks are getting

92
00:03:04,004 --> 00:03:07,234
spoken for… one small slice at a time.

93
00:03:07,493 --> 00:03:08,734
You still feel fine.

94
00:03:08,973 --> 00:03:09,774
Mostly.

95
00:03:10,723 --> 00:03:13,123
Level three. Four plans.

96
00:03:13,143 --> 00:03:15,323
Then it's the big holiday sale.

97
00:03:15,343 --> 00:03:17,913
And everything, everything, has a pay in

98
00:03:17,933 --> 00:03:19,113
four button now.

99
00:03:19,283 --> 00:03:20,823
You're not alone, by the way.

100
00:03:21,003 --> 00:03:22,993
In the twenty twenty-five holiday season,

101
00:03:23,013 --> 00:03:25,773
Americans spent twenty billion dollars online

102
00:03:25,793 --> 00:03:27,313
with buy now, pay later.

103
00:03:27,433 --> 00:03:29,783
And on Cyber Monday alone, it passed one

104
00:03:29,803 --> 00:03:32,083
billion dollars in a single day, for the

105
00:03:32,103 --> 00:03:33,493
first time ever.

106
00:03:33,653 --> 00:03:36,063
Over eighty percent of that was on phones.

107
00:03:36,153 --> 00:03:38,713
Late at night, in bed, one thumb.

108
00:03:38,903 --> 00:03:39,863
Just like you.

109
00:03:40,293 --> 00:03:42,183
And look at how that checkout is built.

110
00:03:42,334 --> 00:03:44,313
The pay later button is bigger than the pay

111
00:03:44,334 --> 00:03:46,703
now button. There's a countdown timer.

112
00:03:46,803 --> 00:03:48,073
"Only three left."

113
00:03:48,263 --> 00:03:50,803
The price in big letters is the small payment,

114
00:03:50,823 --> 00:03:53,633
and the real total is in tiny letters underneath.

115
00:03:53,913 --> 00:03:55,613
None of that is an accident.

116
00:03:55,774 --> 00:03:57,513
Every part of it is designed to make the

117
00:03:57,533 --> 00:03:59,553
yes faster than the thinking.

118
00:03:59,713 --> 00:04:01,593
And it works best on the people who can least

119
00:04:01,613 --> 00:04:04,013
afford it. The Fed's survey found that about

120
00:04:04,033 --> 00:04:06,553
sixteen percent of American adults used buy

121
00:04:06,573 --> 00:04:08,353
now, pay later in a year.

122
00:04:08,413 --> 00:04:10,263
But among households earning twenty-five

123
00:04:10,283 --> 00:04:12,323
to fifty thousand dollars, it was nearly

124
00:04:12,343 --> 00:04:14,973
one in four. Among households earning over

125
00:04:14,993 --> 00:04:17,084
a hundred thousand, it was only about one

126
00:04:17,103 --> 00:04:19,433
in eight. Four plans now.

127
00:04:19,634 --> 00:04:21,853
And you've stopped saying "I'm buying this."

128
00:04:21,943 --> 00:04:24,084
You've started saying "I'm only paying fifty

129
00:04:24,103 --> 00:04:26,564
this week." That's the trick of the whole

130
00:04:26,584 --> 00:04:29,014
thing. The total disappears.

131
00:04:29,103 --> 00:04:30,324
All you ever see…

132
00:04:30,343 --> 00:04:32,233
is the next small payment.

133
00:04:33,233 --> 00:04:35,574
Level four. Seven plans.

134
00:04:35,603 --> 00:04:38,394
Three apps. At some point, you hit the limit

135
00:04:38,413 --> 00:04:39,444
on the first app.

136
00:04:39,554 --> 00:04:41,473
It just won't approve you for more.

137
00:04:41,564 --> 00:04:43,653
So you download a second one.

138
00:04:43,733 --> 00:04:44,963
And then a third.

139
00:04:45,233 --> 00:04:46,304
This has a name.

140
00:04:46,374 --> 00:04:48,163
It's called loan stacking.

141
00:04:48,204 --> 00:04:50,293
And it's way more common than you'd think.

142
00:04:50,554 --> 00:04:52,544
When the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau

143
00:04:52,564 --> 00:04:55,874
studied buy now, pay later loans, about sixty-three

144
00:04:55,894 --> 00:04:58,174
percent of borrowers had more than one loan

145
00:04:58,194 --> 00:05:00,354
going at the same time, at some point during

146
00:05:00,374 --> 00:05:02,674
the year. And a third had borrowed from more

147
00:05:02,694 --> 00:05:05,064
than one buy now, pay later company.

148
00:05:05,184 --> 00:05:07,434
And getting approved again is easy.

149
00:05:07,524 --> 00:05:09,994
That same CFPB study found that in twenty

150
00:05:10,014 --> 00:05:12,254
twenty-two, these companies approved around

151
00:05:12,274 --> 00:05:14,514
seventy-eight percent of loan applications

152
00:05:14,533 --> 00:05:15,484
from people with subprime or deep subprime

153
00:05:15,504 --> 00:05:19,033
credit. The kind of credit that gets a lot

154
00:05:19,053 --> 00:05:21,144
of credit card applications turned down.

155
00:05:21,353 --> 00:05:22,843
Which sounds generous.

156
00:05:22,864 --> 00:05:25,004
But being approved and being able to afford

157
00:05:25,024 --> 00:05:27,283
it are two very different things.

158
00:05:27,514 --> 00:05:28,694
And notice the sofa.

159
00:05:28,824 --> 00:05:30,314
That wasn't pay in four.

160
00:05:30,334 --> 00:05:32,994
That was a monthly plan, a year long.

161
00:05:33,084 --> 00:05:35,344
Some of those longer plans charge interest,

162
00:05:35,364 --> 00:05:36,843
sometimes a lot of it.

163
00:05:36,954 --> 00:05:38,054
The "zero percent"

164
00:05:38,074 --> 00:05:40,084
in the ads doesn't always come along for

165
00:05:40,103 --> 00:05:42,444
the ride. Now here's what makes stacking

166
00:05:42,463 --> 00:05:43,774
so dangerous.

167
00:05:43,913 --> 00:05:46,644
Every app only sees its own plans.

168
00:05:46,744 --> 00:05:48,864
App one doesn't know about app two.

169
00:05:49,014 --> 00:05:51,234
App two doesn't know about app three.

170
00:05:51,303 --> 00:05:53,403
And for a long time, most of this didn't

171
00:05:53,423 --> 00:05:55,384
show up on your credit report either.

172
00:05:55,473 --> 00:05:57,574
Some people call it phantom debt.

173
00:05:57,774 --> 00:06:00,204
It's real money you really owe…

174
00:06:00,274 --> 00:06:03,134
that nobody, including you, can see all in

175
00:06:03,153 --> 00:06:04,254
one place.

176
00:06:05,329 --> 00:06:07,750
Level five. Ten plans.

177
00:06:08,120 --> 00:06:10,290
Then one week, the payments land before your

178
00:06:10,310 --> 00:06:11,370
paycheck does.

179
00:06:11,579 --> 00:06:14,230
The sneakers, the jacket, the headphones,

180
00:06:14,250 --> 00:06:16,810
the chair, the TV, the sofa.

181
00:06:17,069 --> 00:06:18,699
They all want their slice.

182
00:06:19,089 --> 00:06:20,889
And there's not enough left for food.

183
00:06:21,229 --> 00:06:23,179
So you split your groceries.

184
00:06:23,750 --> 00:06:26,240
Stop for a second, because this is the moment

185
00:06:26,259 --> 00:06:27,819
the whole thing changes.

186
00:06:28,120 --> 00:06:30,829
Up to now, buy now, pay later was paying

187
00:06:30,849 --> 00:06:32,269
for things you wanted.

188
00:06:32,500 --> 00:06:34,879
Now it's paying for things you need.

189
00:06:35,109 --> 00:06:36,909
Groceries don't sit in your closet.

190
00:06:36,929 --> 00:06:37,899
You eat them.

191
00:06:38,059 --> 00:06:39,949
And four weeks later, you're still paying

192
00:06:39,969 --> 00:06:41,680
for a meal you had last month…

193
00:06:41,699 --> 00:06:43,969
on top of the meals you need this month.

194
00:06:44,189 --> 00:06:45,990
And this is not some rare thing.

195
00:06:46,219 --> 00:06:48,029
In the Federal Reserve's latest survey of

196
00:06:48,049 --> 00:06:50,620
American households, about one in five buy

197
00:06:50,639 --> 00:06:53,289
now, pay later users had used it for groceries

198
00:06:53,309 --> 00:06:54,469
or food delivery.

199
00:06:54,589 --> 00:06:56,490
And twenty-nine percent said they used it

200
00:06:56,509 --> 00:06:58,470
because it was the only way they could afford

201
00:06:58,490 --> 00:06:59,379
the purchase.

202
00:06:59,719 --> 00:07:01,059
Remember this level.

203
00:07:01,209 --> 00:07:02,449
We're coming back to it.

204
00:07:02,934 --> 00:07:03,954
Quick catch-up.

205
00:07:04,124 --> 00:07:07,244
Level one: one plan, totally fine.

206
00:07:07,544 --> 00:07:10,584
Level two: the plans started overlapping.

207
00:07:10,894 --> 00:07:13,834
Level three: the price tags disappeared.

208
00:07:14,174 --> 00:07:17,004
Level four: three apps, and nobody can see

209
00:07:17,024 --> 00:07:18,104
the whole stack.

210
00:07:18,424 --> 00:07:20,974
Level five: you split your groceries.

211
00:07:21,114 --> 00:07:23,903
So far, nothing has actually gone wrong.

212
00:07:24,074 --> 00:07:25,994
You haven't missed a single payment.

213
00:07:26,204 --> 00:07:27,754
That's about to change.

214
00:07:29,086 --> 00:07:32,076
Level six. The first late payment.

215
00:07:32,367 --> 00:07:33,437
It's a Tuesday.

216
00:07:33,547 --> 00:07:34,847
Payday is Friday.

217
00:07:35,007 --> 00:07:36,787
And at six in the morning, three of your

218
00:07:36,807 --> 00:07:38,757
plans try to take their payments…

219
00:07:38,867 --> 00:07:41,037
from an account that has eleven dollars in

220
00:07:41,057 --> 00:07:43,707
it. The problem is, your plans don't know

221
00:07:43,727 --> 00:07:44,937
when your payday is.

222
00:07:45,137 --> 00:07:47,317
Each one was set up on whatever day you happened

223
00:07:47,336 --> 00:07:48,346
to buy the thing.

224
00:07:48,417 --> 00:07:50,977
So on some weeks, everything lands before

225
00:07:50,997 --> 00:07:52,047
your money does.

226
00:07:52,157 --> 00:07:53,586
Two things happen at once.

227
00:07:53,627 --> 00:07:55,787
The pay later app charges you a late fee.

228
00:07:55,807 --> 00:07:57,917
And your bank charges you an overdraft fee,

229
00:07:57,937 --> 00:07:59,737
because the payment went through anyway and

230
00:07:59,757 --> 00:08:01,206
pushed you below zero.

231
00:08:01,387 --> 00:08:03,457
This is exactly where a lot of people end

232
00:08:03,477 --> 00:08:06,207
up. In that same Federal Reserve survey,

233
00:08:06,227 --> 00:08:08,637
about a quarter of buy now, pay later users

234
00:08:08,657 --> 00:08:10,547
had paid late in the past year.

235
00:08:10,776 --> 00:08:13,347
Of the people who paid late, sixty-four percent

236
00:08:13,367 --> 00:08:14,557
were charged a fee.

237
00:08:14,586 --> 00:08:16,837
And eleven percent of all users got hit with

238
00:08:16,857 --> 00:08:19,487
an overdraft or insufficient-funds fee because

239
00:08:19,507 --> 00:08:21,497
of a buy now, pay later payment.

240
00:08:21,667 --> 00:08:23,597
And notice: the "zero percent"

241
00:08:23,617 --> 00:08:26,817
was only ever true if everything went perfectly.

242
00:08:26,947 --> 00:08:29,766
Every payment, every week, for months.

243
00:08:30,817 --> 00:08:32,826
Real life doesn't go perfectly.

244
00:08:34,015 --> 00:08:37,155
Level seven. Your credit score finds out.

245
00:08:37,485 --> 00:08:39,666
For years, the deal with a lot of these apps

246
00:08:39,686 --> 00:08:42,066
was: they don't really check your credit,

247
00:08:42,086 --> 00:08:44,025
and they don't really report to it.

248
00:08:44,596 --> 00:08:45,666
That's changing.

249
00:08:45,956 --> 00:08:47,885
In twenty twenty-five, one of the biggest

250
00:08:47,905 --> 00:08:50,396
buy now, pay later companies started reporting

251
00:08:50,416 --> 00:08:52,916
all of its loans, including pay in four,

252
00:08:52,936 --> 00:08:55,475
to two of the three major credit bureaus.

253
00:08:55,655 --> 00:08:57,796
And FICO, the company behind most credit

254
00:08:57,816 --> 00:09:00,306
scores, announced new scores built to include

255
00:09:00,326 --> 00:09:02,096
buy now, pay later data.

256
00:09:02,475 --> 00:09:03,775
It's still patchy.

257
00:09:03,865 --> 00:09:06,605
Some companies report, some don't, and lenders

258
00:09:06,625 --> 00:09:08,625
don't all use the newest scores yet.

259
00:09:08,725 --> 00:09:10,556
But the direction is clear.

260
00:09:10,676 --> 00:09:13,225
Your pay later life is becoming visible.

261
00:09:13,495 --> 00:09:15,596
And one thing has always been true.

262
00:09:15,796 --> 00:09:17,716
If a debt goes unpaid long enough to get

263
00:09:17,735 --> 00:09:19,906
sent to collections, that can show up and

264
00:09:19,926 --> 00:09:21,596
drag your credit score down.

265
00:09:21,755 --> 00:09:23,316
The kind of score that decides your rent

266
00:09:23,336 --> 00:09:25,346
applications, your car loan…

267
00:09:25,446 --> 00:09:27,416
sometimes even your phone contract.

268
00:09:27,655 --> 00:09:29,735
And a lower credit score is expensive in

269
00:09:29,755 --> 00:09:31,716
ways you don't see straight away.

270
00:09:31,875 --> 00:09:33,646
It can mean a higher interest rate on a car

271
00:09:33,666 --> 00:09:36,336
loan, a bigger deposit on an apartment, or

272
00:09:36,355 --> 00:09:38,526
a no from a landlord before you've even had

273
00:09:38,546 --> 00:09:40,005
the chance to explain.

274
00:09:41,286 --> 00:09:43,725
Ten plans for sneakers and groceries…

275
00:09:43,875 --> 00:09:45,946
and now it's following you into the rest

276
00:09:45,966 --> 00:09:46,946
of your life.

277
00:09:48,157 --> 00:09:51,147
Level eight. Paying debt with debt.

278
00:09:51,227 --> 00:09:52,987
So you get creative.

279
00:09:53,057 --> 00:09:54,326
Payment due on app one?

280
00:09:54,346 --> 00:09:55,567
Put it on your credit card.

281
00:09:55,586 --> 00:09:56,567
Groceries again?

282
00:09:56,627 --> 00:09:58,017
New plan on app three.

283
00:09:58,147 --> 00:09:58,977
Rent short this month?

284
00:09:58,997 --> 00:10:00,137
Borrow from Jay.

285
00:10:00,157 --> 00:10:01,527
Pay Jay back with…

286
00:10:01,547 --> 00:10:02,507
another plan.

287
00:10:02,767 --> 00:10:04,707
This is the moment the zero percent loan

288
00:10:04,727 --> 00:10:07,507
quietly becomes a very expensive one.

289
00:10:07,596 --> 00:10:09,237
Because the credit card you just used to

290
00:10:09,257 --> 00:10:10,067
cover a "free"

291
00:10:10,086 --> 00:10:12,737
payment? That one does charge interest.

292
00:10:12,757 --> 00:10:14,987
And a lot of it, every month you don't clear

293
00:10:15,007 --> 00:10:17,297
it. Remember lifestyle creep from the side

294
00:10:17,317 --> 00:10:18,257
hustle video?

295
00:10:18,346 --> 00:10:20,326
Each upgrade felt reasonable, until your

296
00:10:20,346 --> 00:10:22,197
life depended on money you didn't really

297
00:10:22,216 --> 00:10:25,737
have. This is the same trap, just faster.

298
00:10:25,757 --> 00:10:27,197
And with more apps.

299
00:10:28,406 --> 00:10:30,576
Level nine. Locked out.

300
00:10:30,946 --> 00:10:33,076
And that brings us back to two in the morning.

301
00:10:33,186 --> 00:10:34,086
Payment failed.

302
00:10:34,105 --> 00:10:34,966
Payment failed.

303
00:10:34,985 --> 00:10:37,985
Late fee. Here's what usually happens next.

304
00:10:38,046 --> 00:10:40,105
Miss payments, and most apps stop letting

305
00:10:40,125 --> 00:10:41,475
you make new purchases.

306
00:10:41,605 --> 00:10:43,125
The thing that was always there to catch

307
00:10:43,145 --> 00:10:44,806
you… isn't anymore.

308
00:10:44,985 --> 00:10:47,355
The plans you already have still need paying.

309
00:10:47,375 --> 00:10:49,225
The fees are still stacking.

310
00:10:49,306 --> 00:10:51,196
And if it goes on long enough, the debt can

311
00:10:51,216 --> 00:10:53,216
be handed to a collections agency.

312
00:10:53,286 --> 00:10:55,066
And that's the version that's most likely

313
00:10:55,086 --> 00:10:56,755
to show up on your credit report.

314
00:10:56,936 --> 00:10:59,166
And honestly, the worst part isn't even the

315
00:10:59,186 --> 00:11:01,046
money. It's the feeling.

316
00:11:01,206 --> 00:11:03,515
Flinching every time your phone buzzes.

317
00:11:03,655 --> 00:11:05,225
Not opening the banking app, because you

318
00:11:05,245 --> 00:11:06,385
don't want to know.

319
00:11:06,526 --> 00:11:08,395
Saying "Yeah, all good"…

320
00:11:08,466 --> 00:11:09,806
when it's really not.

321
00:11:10,326 --> 00:11:11,475
Twelve plans.

322
00:11:11,495 --> 00:11:13,066
Sixty-dollar sneakers.

323
00:11:13,086 --> 00:11:14,956
And a phone you're scared to look at.

324
00:11:15,466 --> 00:11:17,716
Okay. So how do we get out?

325
00:11:18,922 --> 00:11:21,632
Level ten. Zero plans.

326
00:11:21,872 --> 00:11:24,712
Deep breath. Here's how people actually climb

327
00:11:24,732 --> 00:11:28,101
out of this. One: see the whole stack.

328
00:11:28,272 --> 00:11:30,971
Open every app and write down every plan.

329
00:11:31,042 --> 00:11:32,822
What's left, and when it's due.

330
00:11:32,951 --> 00:11:34,732
All of it, in one list.

331
00:11:34,892 --> 00:11:37,272
Phantom debt stops being scary the second

332
00:11:37,292 --> 00:11:38,201
you can see it.

333
00:11:38,542 --> 00:11:40,412
Two: stop adding.

334
00:11:40,532 --> 00:11:42,232
Turn off one-tap checkout.

335
00:11:42,262 --> 00:11:44,492
Remove your saved cards from shopping sites.

336
00:11:44,512 --> 00:11:46,422
Delete the apps you don't need.

337
00:11:46,482 --> 00:11:48,282
Make the next plan harder to start than the

338
00:11:48,302 --> 00:11:51,961
last one. Three: protect the essentials first.

339
00:11:51,992 --> 00:11:54,012
Rent, utilities, food.

340
00:11:54,132 --> 00:11:56,002
Then the plans, starting with whatever is

341
00:11:56,022 --> 00:11:58,162
late or charging fees or interest, because

342
00:11:58,182 --> 00:11:59,481
that's what's growing.

343
00:11:59,812 --> 00:12:01,392
Four: talk to them.

344
00:12:01,552 --> 00:12:03,612
A lot of lenders have hardship options, or

345
00:12:03,632 --> 00:12:05,772
will move a payment date if you ask before

346
00:12:05,792 --> 00:12:08,252
you miss it. And if it's gotten really heavy,

347
00:12:08,272 --> 00:12:10,142
a nonprofit credit counselor can help you

348
00:12:10,162 --> 00:12:11,062
build a plan.

349
00:12:11,292 --> 00:12:13,102
And there are two classic ways to pay the

350
00:12:13,122 --> 00:12:16,082
stack down. The avalanche: put every spare

351
00:12:16,102 --> 00:12:18,122
dollar on whatever's costing you the most

352
00:12:18,142 --> 00:12:20,162
in interest and fees, and pay the minimum

353
00:12:20,182 --> 00:12:21,502
on everything else.

354
00:12:21,652 --> 00:12:24,732
Or the snowball: kill the smallest plan first,

355
00:12:24,752 --> 00:12:26,882
for the quick win, then roll that money into

356
00:12:26,902 --> 00:12:27,822
the next one.

357
00:12:27,961 --> 00:12:30,142
The avalanche usually saves more money.

358
00:12:30,162 --> 00:12:32,412
The snowball usually keeps people going.

359
00:12:32,572 --> 00:12:34,942
The best one is whichever one you'll actually

360
00:12:34,961 --> 00:12:38,142
stick to. And then, one by one, the plans

361
00:12:38,162 --> 00:12:41,692
close. Buy now, pay later isn't evil.

362
00:12:41,721 --> 00:12:43,512
Used once, for something you planned to buy

363
00:12:43,532 --> 00:12:45,932
anyway, with the money already set aside?

364
00:12:45,992 --> 00:12:47,352
It's a free little loan.

365
00:12:47,412 --> 00:12:49,442
The problem was never one plan.

366
00:12:49,512 --> 00:12:50,742
It's the stack.

367
00:12:50,822 --> 00:12:53,002
So if you are going to use it, here are the

368
00:12:53,022 --> 00:12:55,662
rules people who use it safely tend to follow.

369
00:12:55,932 --> 00:12:58,002
Only use it if you could pay the full price

370
00:12:58,022 --> 00:13:00,341
today, from money you already have.

371
00:13:00,502 --> 00:13:01,882
One plan at a time.

372
00:13:01,902 --> 00:13:03,132
Never a stack.

373
00:13:03,172 --> 00:13:05,211
And never for the stuff you eat, the stuff

374
00:13:05,231 --> 00:13:07,322
you need to keep the lights on, or the stuff

375
00:13:07,341 --> 00:13:09,782
you'll be finished with before the last payment.

376
00:13:11,057 --> 00:13:14,357
So. Which purchase was the point of no return?

377
00:13:14,717 --> 00:13:15,857
Not the sneakers.

378
00:13:16,087 --> 00:13:17,977
One plan you can pay off is fine.

379
00:13:18,217 --> 00:13:20,378
Not the TV or the sofa either.

380
00:13:20,467 --> 00:13:22,388
Those hurt, but they were still things you

381
00:13:22,408 --> 00:13:25,447
chose. It was the groceries.

382
00:13:25,587 --> 00:13:28,038
The moment buy now, pay later stopped paying

383
00:13:28,057 --> 00:13:29,957
for things you wanted, and started paying

384
00:13:29,977 --> 00:13:31,347
for things you needed.

385
00:13:31,487 --> 00:13:33,847
Because you can always stop buying sneakers.

386
00:13:33,967 --> 00:13:35,388
You can't stop eating.

387
00:13:35,687 --> 00:13:37,717
From that moment on, every week made the

388
00:13:37,737 --> 00:13:39,148
next week worse.

389
00:13:39,418 --> 00:13:41,748
If you ever catch yourself splitting groceries,

390
00:13:41,768 --> 00:13:43,408
that's not a shortcut anymore.

391
00:13:43,427 --> 00:13:44,807
That's the warning light.

392
00:13:45,057 --> 00:13:46,327
So be honest with me.

393
00:13:46,388 --> 00:13:48,368
How many buy now, pay later plans do you

394
00:13:48,388 --> 00:13:49,587
have open right now?

395
00:13:49,717 --> 00:13:51,878
Zero counts. Tell me in the comments.

396
00:13:51,898 --> 00:13:53,057
I read all of them.

397
00:13:53,258 --> 00:13:54,668
And if you want to see what happens when

398
00:13:54,687 --> 00:13:56,778
you're on the other side of the money, here's

399
00:13:56,798 --> 00:13:59,048
your life at every level of side hustle,

400
00:13:59,067 --> 00:14:01,097
from zero to a million.
